#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065202} :activity/id #uuid "2c535ceb-c030-5aed-b263-486be04ce20c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773 :topic/id #uuid "bd7555e9-f98c-551c-968c-84222eeed14d" :topic/math-academy-id 294} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065395 :tutorial/content "**Logarithmic differentiation** is a technique to compute derivatives that are difficult to find using the usual rules of differentiation, such as the product, quotient, and chain rules.\n\nLet's consider the following function.\n\n$$\ny = x^x\n$$\n\nWe aim to compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nHowever, in this case, we note the following:\n\n- Both the base and the exponent are functions of $x$.\n- This means we cannot apply the power rule since the exponent is not a fixed number.\n- We also cannot apply the rules for differentiating exponentials since the base is not a fixed number.\n\nSo instead, we'll use logarithmic differentiation. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{x} \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln x^{x} \\\\\n\\ln y &= x\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\cdot \\ln x + x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 \\cdot \\ln x + x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\ln x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Now, we solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y\\left(\\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y=x^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^x \\left(\\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "21321352-0483-513c-828f-f1c46306015a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6087 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9aa16304-115f-5e48-8777-8b493acf1b51" :step/math-academy-id 37025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064143 :question/id #uuid "a369d9b2-7637-5339-bd75-5f49b1aac962" :question/math-academy-id "e-9842"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424278}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "5b0b9f01-a558-5a72-80f9-542d588ba830" :step/math-academy-id 37026 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064144 :question/id #uuid "bdccebc1-05df-5c7b-a57b-fa50df42a32e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9843"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495050}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"} :step/id #uuid "2ed78f46-95a3-57ea-b05a-7c9ceb493d71" :step/math-academy-id 37027 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064145 :question/id #uuid "4d047c1a-308b-553b-89e4-9afc5cc9f766" :question/math-academy-id "e-9844"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424279}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "8d9521b5-8275-530d-9b3c-7e382a4d4606" :step/math-academy-id 37028}] :activity/title "Logarithmic Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064398}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413631} :activity/id #uuid "8ddfa349-33f9-52db-ae99-fde6c1886942" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773 :topic/id #uuid "bd7555e9-f98c-551c-968c-84222eeed14d" :topic/math-academy-id 294} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b2dd31-87ef-57c3-a041-f20e700d80c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413608} {:answer/id #uuid "13f20f22-f41f-5f1b-8d9d-f2adb8019550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413609} {:answer/id #uuid "21ede1b3-ec6b-56c3-a5bd-57ae68918058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413610} {:answer/id #uuid "a22eeb7f-6011-5b02-bcc9-4fb5207df252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413615} {:answer/id #uuid "f63be48c-a2c8-558a-af22-1c2d536b7b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413615} :answer-field/id #uuid "67f1cc61-ae77-5747-982e-80a1c789aa8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4ed5979-35bd-5227-b6d2-7a9f08a0cbdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-138812" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$"} :step/id #uuid "2312e57b-a556-5b41-9d0e-0eea036d1a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96beea54-57c3-53ca-9a62-26604472224a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\csc(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413614} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b8438b-3327-575c-ae05-92d222d1ed04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fed635cb-d378-5d78-b478-1026e862dee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-341953" :question/problem "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(x))) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{csc}^{2}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)})}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "677687e2-cbea-59cc-ba58-b6532d7f53b5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bc056c-eb5f-5687-8409-0974c7956f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x+3)\\ln(2x+3)+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413612} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44a882d-c0e6-5d33-a2fe-067332daaf5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31fc854a-70c5-5387-9f4b-327eb3d213ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-341727" :question/problem "Given that $y=(2x+3)^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(2x+3)^{x-1}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x+3)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x+3)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x+3}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(2x+3)^{x}:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+3)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}) \\\\  & =(2x+3)^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)}{2x+3}+\\frac{2x}{2x+3}) \\\\  & =(2x+3)^{x-1}\\cdot ((2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+3)+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7e5a5f59-c844-5794-bc23-ded640c9e69a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228d9dea-2b4e-5f17-8cd2-d73751d80342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413611} {:answer/id #uuid "602de7c1-ef38-5158-8b6f-d070c0442d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413613} {:answer/id #uuid "df0b2f5d-7c7c-5292-bd94-2ac5f79bd226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413616} {:answer/id #uuid "e31a12e8-2630-53d1-8e4f-075fa6dd173e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413618} {:answer/id #uuid "e984a0af-51e1-5904-82b6-34c5f776d3f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413611} :answer-field/id #uuid "795f926a-e3fd-544a-ba60-66f3eb632b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a381386-3c00-511f-88a6-9a57fbf09d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138855" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+{cot}^{2}⁡x.$"} :step/id #uuid "dc6881df-ed35-5418-89e0-60e2a598bae4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e24a86b9-7405-5dbf-acfb-1af04c6cd34f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413617} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f8ee9f-a89b-56b6-9a08-fe333af36907" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d2d4364-ca99-526d-979f-b7f7eb9eac46" :question/math-academy-id "q-341945" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+1)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2x+1}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y\\,(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=0} & ={(\\sqrt{2(0)+1})}^{(0)^{2}+1}(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)+1)+\\frac{(0)^{2}+1}{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1b8336d-b863-5eac-890e-6cb97f6a293f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413632}}] :activity/title "Logarithmic Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413607}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424281} :activity/id #uuid "eccafe71-d393-5867-ad3d-6f075e8d14e5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773 :topic/id #uuid "bd7555e9-f98c-551c-968c-84222eeed14d" :topic/math-academy-id 294} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f33a63-c291-5841-be1b-4faff432cabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424260} {:answer/id #uuid "438050ca-5554-5efb-b6ab-495501b5ed22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424262} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4c6e33-c46b-5d45-876e-4d7c5861b00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424270} {:answer/id #uuid "e7caf4ba-0364-56de-9d87-fa137b1cb670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424271} {:answer/id #uuid "fd40483e-6c18-50b5-b937-12944951fb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424271} :answer-field/id #uuid "4522aec1-18fc-5a98-a19b-f2258fae7499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09ef39a3-aa47-5850-a2b1-1c368b15a626" :question/math-academy-id "q-2523" :question/problem "Consider the function $y={x}^{(2x+1)}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2x+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2x+1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{2x+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2x+1}(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x+1}(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}\\cdot x(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}(2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1.$"} :step/id #uuid "4bf48ceb-23da-56bd-b48d-6f977a912d9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090603b9-f435-5f7d-81b3-5fa7d70d5ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424261} {:answer/id #uuid "4902eb83-e8de-509d-9be7-218c3d06e15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424263} {:answer/id #uuid "57d95622-82b3-5393-b370-8d92f43e6f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424264} {:answer/id #uuid "60eefb25-daf9-5e08-b4ef-37256dd0bc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424265} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdefb81-a578-5bb1-b28c-f155bdc49d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424261} :answer-field/id #uuid "1814a4c4-4f4b-5074-b701-a59013925f9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc4aa46c-af83-51d5-a531-dab6715c6436" :question/math-academy-id "q-138807" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(3{x}^{2})^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(3{x}^{2})^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(3{x}^{2})^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3{x}^{2})^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot 6x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(3{x}^{2})^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(3{x}^{2})^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{x}^{2})+2.$"} :step/id #uuid "865f7695-0213-516c-98bf-462e100e7f3a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ebb2cb7-894c-5ea1-8fe9-5d932e0eaec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424266} {:answer/id #uuid "bc944f96-6c8b-5c8c-9ec9-40c54f081a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424267} {:answer/id #uuid "c290b836-3e40-5e91-84ff-1cbd84d8ae28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424269} {:answer/id #uuid "e80dcef6-8cf3-58d4-8627-98be0f13b772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424272} {:answer/id #uuid "ead5149a-8ad0-50cd-aebf-6ea20a3a902d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424273} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da65985-bad5-5a30-9563-e72ed071e141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82f90d91-406e-5d3c-bce9-9c8235648ab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138851" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(2x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(2x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$"} :step/id #uuid "ba697e86-52af-5b67-9f61-f507a6024c3c"}] :activity/title "Logarithmic Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424259}]}
